                    <h1 class="content-title">Risk Management <span class="gradient-text">Lifecycle</span></h1>
                    <p class="content-subtitle">
                        Every risk in IRM follows a structured lifecycle. This page walks through each state, 
                        how assessments work, scoring methodologies, and response workflows.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- Risk States -->
                <section class="content-section" id="risk-states">
                    <h2>Risk Lifecycle States</h2>
                    <p>
                        A risk record in the Advanced Risk application goes through a defined state model. 
                        Each transition can be controlled by business rules, approvals, and conditions.
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Risk State Flow</div>
                        <div class="flow-container">
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">01</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Draft</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Initial creation in register</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">02</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Assess</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">RAM methodology applied</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">03</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Respond</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Treatment strategy selected</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">04</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Monitor</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Continuous tracking via indicators</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">05</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Retired</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Risk no longer applicable</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>State Details</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Draft:</strong> The risk record is created in the risk register. Key fields are 
                            populated — Name, Description, Entity (Profile), Owner, and Risk Category. The risk exists 
                            but hasn't been formally assessed yet.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Assess:</strong> The Risk Assessment Methodology (RAM) is applied to evaluate the risk. 
                            Assessment factors (both manual and automated) are evaluated to determine inherent risk scores. 
                            This is where the ARA framework comes into play.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Respond:</strong> Triggered once assessment is complete, especially when residual risk 
                            exceeds the defined risk appetite. The risk owner selects a response strategy and creates 
                            treatment tasks.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Monitor:</strong> The risk is actively monitored through indicators and periodic 
                            reassessments. Changes in control effectiveness or new threat information can trigger 
                            re-assessment.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Retired:</strong> The risk is no longer relevant — perhaps the asset was decommissioned, 
                            the threat was eliminated, or the risk was fully transferred.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <!-- ARA -->
                <section class="content-section" id="ara">
                    <h2>Advanced Risk Assessment (ARA)</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Advanced Risk Assessment</strong> is the modern assessment framework in IRM, replacing 
                        the legacy "Classic" assessment approach. ARA provides much more flexibility and configurability 
                        for defining how risks are assessed.
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Migration Note</div>
                        <p>
                            ARA must be enabled by setting the "Migrate to Advanced Risk Assessments" system property. 
                            Once enabled, the system uses the new RAM-based assessment engine. This is a one-way migration 
                            — ensure you understand the implications before enabling in production.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <h3>ARA Assessment Types</h3>
                    <p>ARA supports multiple assessment types that can be run independently or in sequence:</p>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Assessment Type</th>
                                    <th>What It Measures</th>
                                    <th>When to Use</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Inherent</strong></td>
                                    <td>Risk level without any controls in place</td>
                                    <td>Initial risk identification, before controls are applied</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Control Effectiveness</strong></td>
                                    <td>How well existing controls mitigate the risk</td>
                                    <td>After controls are implemented, during ongoing monitoring</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Residual</strong></td>
                                    <td>Risk level after controls are applied</td>
                                    <td>To determine if remaining risk is acceptable</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Target</strong></td>
                                    <td>Desired risk level after treatment plans</td>
                                    <td>When planning additional risk responses</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- RAM -->
                <section class="content-section" id="ram">
                    <h2>Risk Assessment Methodology (RAM)</h2>
                    <p>
                        The <strong>RAM</strong> is the configurable blueprint that defines <em>how</em> risks are 
                        assessed across your organization. Think of it as a "recipe" for risk assessment — it specifies 
                        what questions to ask, how to score answers, and how to aggregate results.
                    </p>

                    <h3>RAM Components</h3>
                    <div class="accordion">
                        <div class="accordion-item open">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger">
                                <span class="accordion-trigger-text">Assessment Context</span>
                                <span class="accordion-icon">▾</span>
                            </button>
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                <div class="accordion-content">
                                    <p>
                                        Defines <strong>what</strong> is being assessed — the scope of the assessment. 
                                        This determines which entities, risk statements, or categories are included. 
                                        The context can be set to target specific entity types, risk categories, or 
                                        individual risk records.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger">
                                <span class="accordion-trigger-text">Factors</span>
                                <span class="accordion-icon">▾</span>
                            </button>
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                <div class="accordion-content">
                                    <p>
                                        Factors are the individual questions or data points used for scoring. They come 
                                        in two types:
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        <strong>Manual Factors:</strong> Judgment-based questions answered by risk owners 
                                        or assessors (e.g., "How likely is this risk to materialize in the next 12 months?").
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        <strong>Automated Factors:</strong> System-derived data pulled from other ServiceNow 
                                        records (e.g., number of unpatched vulnerabilities, incident frequency). These enable 
                                        continuous, data-driven risk assessment without human intervention.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger">
                                <span class="accordion-trigger-text">Scoring & Rating Criteria</span>
                                <span class="accordion-icon">▾</span>
                            </button>
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                <div class="accordion-content">
                                    <p>
                                        Configures how factor responses are converted to numerical scores and then mapped 
                                        to qualitative ratings (Low, Medium, High, Critical). You define:
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        • <strong>Score ranges:</strong> What numerical range maps to each rating level<br>
                                        • <strong>Factor weights:</strong> How much each factor contributes to the overall score<br>
                                        • <strong>Aggregation method:</strong> How multiple factor scores combine (average, weighted average, max)
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger">
                                <span class="accordion-trigger-text">Roll-up Preferences</span>
                                <span class="accordion-icon">▾</span>
                            </button>
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                <div class="accordion-content">
                                    <p>
                                        Determines how individual risk scores aggregate up through entity or risk hierarchies. 
                                        For example, if an entity has 5 associated risks, the roll-up preference defines how 
                                        those 5 scores combine to create an entity-level risk score. Options include maximum 
                                        value, average, weighted sum, or custom formulas.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Scoring -->
                <section class="content-section" id="scoring">
                    <h2>Scoring & Rating</h2>
                    <p>
                        Risk scoring in IRM follows a structured process. Understanding the flow from factor 
                        responses to final risk ratings is essential for configuration and troubleshooting.
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Scoring Flow</div>
                        <div class="flow-container">
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Factors</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Individual questions / data points</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Factor Scores</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Numeric values per factor</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Weighted Aggregate</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Combined using weights & method</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Risk Rating</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Low / Medium / High / Critical</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Developer Tip</div>
                        <p>
                            The formula <strong>Residual Risk = Inherent Risk × (1 - Control Effectiveness)</strong> is 
                            a common starting point, but in ARA, this relationship is fully configurable through the RAM. 
                            You can define custom formulas that factor in additional dimensions like velocity, 
                            detectability, or operational impact.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Risk Response -->
                <section class="content-section" id="risk-response">
                    <h2>Risk Response</h2>
                    <p>
                        When a risk assessment is complete and the residual risk exceeds acceptable levels, 
                        the risk moves to the <strong>Respond</strong> state. The risk owner must select a 
                        treatment strategy:
                    </p>

                    <div class="tabs-container">
                        <div class="tabs-header">
                            <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-mitigate">Mitigate</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-avoid">Avoid</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-transfer">Transfer</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-accept">Accept</button>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-mitigate">
                            <h3>Mitigate</h3>
                            <p>
                                Implement additional controls or strengthen existing ones to reduce the risk to 
                                acceptable levels. This is the most common response strategy. Mitigation tasks 
                                are created and assigned to control owners, and the risk is reassessed after 
                                implementation.
                            </p>
                            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Adding multi-factor authentication to reduce unauthorized access risk.</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-avoid">
                            <h3>Avoid</h3>
                            <p>
                                Eliminate the activity or condition that gives rise to the risk. This is 
                                appropriate when the risk outweighs the benefit of the activity entirely.
                            </p>
                            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Discontinuing use of an end-of-life application that poses security risks.</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-transfer">
                            <h3>Transfer</h3>
                            <p>
                                Shift the risk to a third party, typically through insurance, contracts, or 
                                outsourcing. The risk still exists but the financial or operational impact 
                                is borne by another entity.
                            </p>
                            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Purchasing cyber insurance for data breach liability.</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-accept">
                            <h3>Accept</h3>
                            <p>
                                Acknowledge the risk and take no additional action. This is appropriate when 
                                the cost of treatment exceeds the potential impact, or when the risk is within 
                                the organization's risk appetite. Typically requires formal approval.
                            </p>
                            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Accepting a low-probability operational risk for a non-critical system.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Approval Workflows</div>
                        <p>
                            Risk responses can be configured with multi-level approval workflows using the 
                            <strong>GRC Approval Configurator</strong>. You can set responses to trigger 
                            automatically based on risk appetite status or require manual approval from 
                            designated authorities.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Risk Appetite -->
                <section class="content-section" id="risk-appetite">
                    <h2>Risk Appetite</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Risk Appetite</strong> defines the level of risk an organization is willing to 
                        accept. In IRM, risk appetite is configured as thresholds that automatically trigger 
                        actions when exceeded:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Within Appetite:</strong> Risk is acceptable — no response required</li>
                        <li><strong>Approaching Limit:</strong> Warning zone — review recommended</li>
                        <li><strong>Over Appetite:</strong> Risk exceeds tolerance — response required, approval workflows triggered</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>
                        Risk appetite can be set at different levels — organizational, entity type, or individual 
                        entity. This hierarchical approach lets you apply stricter thresholds to critical systems 
                        while allowing more tolerance for lower-impact areas.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- RCSA -->
                <section class="content-section" id="rcsa">
                    <h2>RCSA Projects</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Risk & Control Self-Assessment (RCSA)</strong> is a structured process where 
                        business units assess their own risks and controls. In IRM, RCSA is managed through 
                        <strong>Risk Assessment Projects</strong> in the Risk Workspace.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        RCSA Projects allow simultaneous assessment of multiple risks and controls across 
                        multiple entities. This is particularly useful for:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Annual or quarterly risk assessments across departments</li>
                        <li>Post-incident reassessment of affected areas</li>
                        <li>New regulation impact assessment across the organization</li>
                        <li>Merger & acquisition risk evaluation</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

